1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,530
Two hundred and forty-three.

2
00:00:01,580 --> 00:00:05,430
That's how close BYD came to beating
Toyota in Australia in June 2026.

3
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,190
Two hundred and forty-three cars,

4
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:09,290
in a single month,

5
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:12,810
separating the most powerful car brand
in the country from a Chinese company

6
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:15,850
most Australians hadn't
heard of five years ago.

7
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:20,430
Toyota has been Australia's best-selling
brand since 2003 the year it overtook

8
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:21,050
Holden.

9
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:21,750
Holden.

10
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,890
The brand that was once as
Australian as meat pies and footy.

11
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:28,250
Seventeen years after Toyota
passed it, Holden was gone.

12
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:30,270
Now Toyota's own numbers are falling.

13
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:34,650
Its sales were down almost a quarter
in the first five months of 2026.

14
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,990
The Prado, the Kluger,
the Yaris down forty,

15
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,610
fifty, even seventy per
cent in some months.

16
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:43,870
And one of Australia's best-known
motoring journalists wrote a column with a

17
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,010
headline that would have been
unthinkable a few years ago:

18
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:51,210
"Toyota is the new Holden not
in a good way." So is it true?

19
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:53,910
Is Toyota really heading
down the same road as Holden?

20
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:56,270
In this video, I'll show
you the real numbers,

21
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,050
what Toyota says is going on,

22
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,590
what actually killed Holden and the
warning signs to watch from here.

23
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:02,430
And by the end,

24
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:06,480
you'll know whether your Toyota is still
the safe bet everyone thinks it is.

25
00:01:06,991 --> 00:01:10,321
First, you need to understand
just how dominant Toyota has been.

26
00:01:10,371 --> 00:01:16,821
In 2024, Toyota sold 241,296
new vehicles in Australia

27
00:01:16,871 --> 00:01:18,121
an all-time record.

28
00:01:18,171 --> 00:01:22,401
In 2025, almost one in every five new
vehicles sold in this country was a

29
00:01:22,451 --> 00:01:23,281
Toyota.

30
00:01:23,331 --> 00:01:26,061
Its market share was
above twenty per cent.

31
00:01:26,111 --> 00:01:29,521
Toyota's rise is one of the great
business stories in Australian history.

32
00:01:29,571 --> 00:01:31,261
It built cars here for decades,

33
00:01:31,311 --> 00:01:33,621
including the Camry at
its factory in Altona,

34
00:01:33,671 --> 00:01:37,361
Victoria, until local
manufacturing ended in 2017.

35
00:01:37,411 --> 00:01:41,311
It took the market lead from Holden
in 2003 and never gave it back.

36
00:01:41,361 --> 00:01:43,941
For more than twenty
years, through recessions,

37
00:01:43,991 --> 00:01:46,261
a pandemic and a supply crisis,

38
00:01:46,311 --> 00:01:50,201
Toyota has finished every single year
as Australia's number one car brand.

39
00:01:50,251 --> 00:01:51,361
Think about the names.

40
00:01:51,411 --> 00:01:55,031
The HiLux, which for years fought the
Ford Ranger for the title of Australia's

41
00:01:55,081 --> 00:01:56,421
best-selling vehicle.

42
00:01:56,471 --> 00:01:59,801
The RAV4, the country's
favourite family SUV.

43
00:01:59,851 --> 00:02:02,521
The LandCruiser, a legend
from the outback to the city.

44
00:02:02,571 --> 00:02:06,141
The Prado, the Corolla,
the Camry, the Kluger.

45
00:02:06,191 --> 00:02:10,081
For millions of Australians, buying
a Toyota wasn't just a choice.

46
00:02:10,131 --> 00:02:12,401
It was the default the
car that held its value,

47
00:02:12,451 --> 00:02:15,881
never let you down and could
be fixed in any country town.

48
00:02:15,931 --> 00:02:18,311
That reputation shows up
in the resale numbers.

49
00:02:18,361 --> 00:02:24,101
A 2025 analysis by Pickles and Datium
Insights found a two-year-old RAV4 kept

50
00:02:24,151 --> 00:02:27,761
about 88 per cent of its value one
of the best results of any car in

51
00:02:27,811 --> 00:02:28,101
Australia.

52
00:02:28,151 --> 00:02:33,221
The LandCruiser also kept around 88
per cent, and the HiLux about 85.

53
00:02:33,271 --> 00:02:34,981
Toyota didn't just lead the market.

54
00:02:35,031 --> 00:02:36,341
It was the market.

55
00:02:36,391 --> 00:02:40,231
And that's exactly why what happened
in 2026 has shocked the industry.

56
00:02:41,069 --> 00:02:43,779
Here's the slide, in Toyota's own numbers.

57
00:02:43,829 --> 00:02:46,019
To the end of May 2026,

58
00:02:46,069 --> 00:02:52,159
Toyota delivered 76,017 vehicles
in Australia down 24.6 per cent on

59
00:02:52,209 --> 00:02:54,539
the same period in 2025.

60
00:02:54,589 --> 00:02:58,999
Its share of the new-car market fell
from 20.7 per cent to 16 per cent.

61
00:02:59,049 --> 00:03:02,579
In May alone, Toyota sales
were down almost 31 per cent.

62
00:03:02,629 --> 00:03:04,539
And it was across the range.

63
00:03:04,589 --> 00:03:06,819
According to CarExpert, by the end of May,

64
00:03:06,869 --> 00:03:09,819
the LandCruiser Prado was
down 45 per cent for the year,

65
00:03:09,869 --> 00:03:12,619
and in May itself it
fell almost 64 per cent.

66
00:03:12,669 --> 00:03:17,739
The Kluger was down almost 40 per cent
for the year, and 70.7 per cent in May.

67
00:03:17,789 --> 00:03:20,139
The Yaris was down 44.4 per cent.

68
00:03:20,189 --> 00:03:22,819
The Yaris Cross, almost 32.

69
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:26,239
RAV4 sales were down 47
per cent year to date.

70
00:03:26,289 --> 00:03:28,419
Even the HiLux was down 13 per cent.

71
00:03:28,469 --> 00:03:32,019
And LandCruiser 70 Series ute
sales were down 82 per cent.

72
00:03:32,069 --> 00:03:34,399
Toyota's explanation for
the Prado was simple.

73
00:03:34,449 --> 00:03:36,319
A spokesperson said:

74
00:03:36,369 --> 00:03:40,399
"The Prado May result reflects lower
supply on this very popular model." And

75
00:03:40,449 --> 00:03:41,759
not everything was falling.

76
00:03:41,809 --> 00:03:46,259
CarExpert reported that LandCruiser
300 and 70 Series wagon sales combined

77
00:03:46,309 --> 00:03:50,739
were actually up 29.3 per cent over
the first five months of the year.

78
00:03:50,789 --> 00:03:54,699
In other words, where Toyota could get
cars, people were still buying them.

79
00:03:54,749 --> 00:03:58,339
By September, things had improved,
but the year is still well down.

80
00:03:58,389 --> 00:04:03,559
Year to date, Toyota has
sold 152,944 vehicles,

81
00:04:03,609 --> 00:04:05,099
down 15.9 per cent.

82
00:04:05,149 --> 00:04:10,599
Its market share is 16.7 per cent,
down from 19.9 a year earlier.

83
00:04:10,649 --> 00:04:14,739
Toyota is still comfortably number one
but it's selling almost 29,000 fewer

84
00:04:14,789 --> 00:04:17,259
cars than it did at the
same point last year.

85
00:04:17,309 --> 00:04:19,599
For any other brand,
that would be a bad year.

86
00:04:19,649 --> 00:04:21,549
For Toyota, it's a historic one.

87
00:04:22,284 --> 00:04:25,524
And while Toyota was falling,
someone else was rising very fast.

88
00:04:25,574 --> 00:04:30,624
In June 2026, Toyota sold 19,124 vehicles.

89
00:04:30,674 --> 00:04:34,334
BYD sold 18,881.

90
00:04:34,384 --> 00:04:36,994
The gap was just 243 cars.

91
00:04:37,044 --> 00:04:40,254
Toyota held 13.7 per cent
of the market that month.

92
00:04:40,304 --> 00:04:42,594
BYD had 13.5.

93
00:04:42,644 --> 00:04:46,594
A Chinese brand that only started selling
cars here a few years ago was almost

94
00:04:46,644 --> 00:04:49,434
level with the most powerful
car company in Australia.

95
00:04:49,484 --> 00:04:51,634
The head of the Federal Chamber
of Automotive Industries,

96
00:04:51,684 --> 00:04:52,914
Tony Weber, said:

97
00:04:52,964 --> 00:04:56,574
"The Australian automotive market has
shifted on its axis during the first

98
00:04:56,624 --> 00:05:00,354
months of 2026." BYD's
chief operating officer,

99
00:05:00,404 --> 00:05:03,494
Stephen Collins, said: "We asked
our customers to back us in,

100
00:05:03,544 --> 00:05:05,814
and they did, and we delivered." Now,

101
00:05:05,864 --> 00:05:08,854
to be fair, BYD hasn't
caught Toyota since.

102
00:05:08,904 --> 00:05:14,961
In September, Toyota sold
17,682 vehicles to BYD's

103
00:05:15,011 --> 00:05:16,034
8,191.

104
00:05:16,084 --> 00:05:19,174
Year to date, Toyota has sold
roughly double BYD's total.

105
00:05:19,224 --> 00:05:23,994
But BYD's growth up 102 per cent this
year shows how quickly the ground can

106
00:05:24,044 --> 00:05:24,634
move.

107
00:05:24,684 --> 00:05:26,321
And it isn't only BYD.

108
00:05:26,371 --> 00:05:29,994
Chinese-built cars now make up almost
four in ten new vehicles sold in

109
00:05:30,044 --> 00:05:31,014
Australia.

110
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:34,854
China overtook Japan as Australia's
biggest source of new cars in February

111
00:05:34,904 --> 00:05:38,754
2026 for the first time since 1998.

112
00:05:38,804 --> 00:05:42,754
Most of the sales Chinese brands have
gained have come from Japanese brands.

113
00:05:42,804 --> 00:05:45,354
And the biggest Japanese
brand of all is Toyota.

114
00:05:45,404 --> 00:05:47,794
But here's an important
detail that often gets lost.

115
00:05:47,844 --> 00:05:51,934
Toyota is actually doing better
than most of its Japanese rivals.

116
00:05:51,984 --> 00:05:54,734
Year to date, Toyota
is down 15.9 per cent.

117
00:05:54,784 --> 00:05:57,054
Mazda is down 16.2.

118
00:05:57,104 --> 00:05:58,801
Mitsubishi is down 30.2.

119
00:05:58,851 --> 00:06:01,494
Subaru is down 29.3.

120
00:06:01,544 --> 00:06:03,914
And Nissan is down 33.3 per cent.

121
00:06:03,964 --> 00:06:05,594
If Toyota is in trouble,

122
00:06:05,644 --> 00:06:09,044
the rest of Japan's car industry in
Australia is in much deeper trouble.

123
00:06:09,811 --> 00:06:12,601
To understand why people are
comparing Toyota to Holden,

124
00:06:12,651 --> 00:06:14,291
you need to remember how Holden fell.

125
00:06:14,341 --> 00:06:18,301
Holden was the pride of Australian
motoring for most of the twentieth century.

126
00:06:18,351 --> 00:06:21,321
The Commodore was the country's
favourite car for years.

127
00:06:21,371 --> 00:06:24,021
Holden and Ford's
rivalry defined Bathurst,

128
00:06:24,071 --> 00:06:26,581
and generations of families
drove nothing else.

129
00:06:26,631 --> 00:06:31,561
But in 2003, Toyota overtook Holden
as Australia's top-selling car maker.

130
00:06:31,611 --> 00:06:33,701
Holden kept selling large numbers of cars,

131
00:06:33,751 --> 00:06:36,301
but buyers were moving to SUVs and utes,

132
00:06:36,351 --> 00:06:39,121
and to Japanese, Korean
and then Chinese brands.

133
00:06:39,171 --> 00:06:42,621
In 2013, Holden announced it
would end local manufacturing.

134
00:06:42,671 --> 00:06:47,201
The last Australian-built Commodore
rolled off the line in 2017.

135
00:06:47,251 --> 00:06:50,521
The imported cars that replaced it
never caught on in the same way.

136
00:06:50,571 --> 00:06:53,621
Then, on the 17th of February 2020,

137
00:06:53,671 --> 00:06:56,961
General Motors announced it would
retire the Holden brand altogether.

138
00:06:57,011 --> 00:06:59,001
GM's international operations boss,

139
00:06:59,051 --> 00:07:02,721
Julian Blissett, pointed to "highly
fragmented right-hand-drive markets",

140
00:07:02,771 --> 00:07:06,181
and said GM "regret[s] that we could
not prioritize the investment required

141
00:07:06,231 --> 00:07:07,881
for Holden" to succeed.

142
00:07:07,931 --> 00:07:10,181
GM called the decision "agonizing".

143
00:07:10,231 --> 00:07:12,471
By the end of 2020, Holden was gone.

144
00:07:12,521 --> 00:07:15,221
The lesson most people
take from Holden is this:

145
00:07:15,271 --> 00:07:19,021
a famous badge can carry a brand
for a long time but not forever.

146
00:07:19,071 --> 00:07:21,301
When the products stop
matching what buyers want,

147
00:07:21,351 --> 00:07:24,921
loyalty runs out, and it can run
out faster than anyone expects.

148
00:07:24,971 --> 00:07:28,001
It's worth remembering that
Holden's decline wasn't sudden,

149
00:07:28,051 --> 00:07:29,321
even if the end was.

150
00:07:29,371 --> 00:07:31,261
For years, the warning signs were there:

151
00:07:31,311 --> 00:07:34,401
falling market share, fewer
models that buyers really wanted,

152
00:07:34,451 --> 00:07:36,241
and a parent company
that was losing interest.

153
00:07:36,291 --> 00:07:37,781
At each step,

154
00:07:37,831 --> 00:07:41,761
plenty of people said Holden was too
big and too Australian to disappear.

155
00:07:41,811 --> 00:07:42,731
Until it did.

156
00:07:43,485 --> 00:07:46,855
Quick favour before we get to Toyota's
side of the story because it's a lot

157
00:07:46,905 --> 00:07:48,975
more convincing than you might think.

158
00:07:49,025 --> 00:07:50,255
Do you own a Toyota?

159
00:07:50,305 --> 00:07:53,235
Would you buy another one, or are
you looking at something else now?

160
00:07:53,285 --> 00:07:54,535
Tell me in the comments.

161
00:07:54,585 --> 00:07:55,815
And if you want straight-talking,

162
00:07:55,865 --> 00:07:58,855
numbers-first videos about what's
really happening in the Australian car

163
00:07:58,905 --> 00:08:00,455
market, hit subscribe.

164
00:08:00,505 --> 00:08:02,285
It's free and it really helps.

165
00:08:03,076 --> 00:08:05,166
So, is Toyota the next Holden?

166
00:08:05,216 --> 00:08:08,566
Let's start with the
case for the prosecution.

167
00:08:08,616 --> 00:08:09,666
In May 2026,

168
00:08:09,716 --> 00:08:13,786
CarsGuide's Stephen Ottley wrote
the column that started this debate.

169
00:08:13,836 --> 00:08:17,806
His argument was that Toyota risks
repeating Holden's mistakes relying on its

170
00:08:17,856 --> 00:08:21,146
badge and on updated versions
of familiar nameplates,

171
00:08:21,196 --> 00:08:24,446
while Chinese brands pour into the
market with all-new cars at a ferocious

172
00:08:24,496 --> 00:08:25,466
pace.

173
00:08:25,516 --> 00:08:28,786
He wrote that "there are no guarantees
in this business and fortunes can

174
00:08:28,836 --> 00:08:33,506
suddenly change for even the biggest
brand." There's evidence to support that

175
00:08:33,556 --> 00:08:34,036
worry.

176
00:08:34,086 --> 00:08:38,106
Chinese brands now offer far more
electric and plug-in hybrid choices,

177
00:08:38,156 --> 00:08:41,146
often with more power and
more equipment for less money.

178
00:08:41,196 --> 00:08:45,386
The number of Chinese models on sale in
Australia is expected to grow from 72

179
00:08:45,436 --> 00:08:48,646
to around 120 by 2027.

180
00:08:48,696 --> 00:08:52,786
And the market isn't growing total
sales for the first nine months of 2026

181
00:08:52,836 --> 00:08:54,926
were almost exactly flat.

182
00:08:54,976 --> 00:08:57,746
Every new rival's sale
is coming from someone.

183
00:08:57,796 --> 00:08:58,886
Price is a big part of it.

184
00:08:58,936 --> 00:09:05,506
The cheapest RAV4 plug-in hybrid
starts at $58,840 before on-road

185
00:09:05,556 --> 00:09:06,586
costs.

186
00:09:06,636 --> 00:09:12,746
GWM's new Haval H7 plug-in hybrid
launched at $49,990 drive-away

187
00:09:12,796 --> 00:09:16,846
with more power, all-wheel drive
and a longer claimed electric range.

188
00:09:16,896 --> 00:09:19,986
Toyota buyers have always been willing
to pay a little more for the badge and

189
00:09:20,036 --> 00:09:20,946
the resale.

190
00:09:20,996 --> 00:09:24,626
The question is how much more and
whether that gap is now too big.

191
00:09:24,676 --> 00:09:25,486
Then there's regulation.

192
00:09:25,536 --> 00:09:28,126
Under the New Vehicle Efficiency Standard,

193
00:09:28,176 --> 00:09:32,506
the emissions target for utes
and vans drops sharply in 2027.

194
00:09:32,556 --> 00:09:34,366
Toyota's sales boss, John Pappas,

195
00:09:34,416 --> 00:09:36,686
has called those targets
"really challenging",

196
00:09:36,736 --> 00:09:39,886
and Toyota has said it "cannot
rule out" price increases.

197
00:09:39,936 --> 00:09:43,466
For a brand that sells huge numbers
of diesel utes and four-wheel drives,

198
00:09:43,516 --> 00:09:44,686
that's a real risk.

199
00:09:44,736 --> 00:09:47,426
And there's the question of
whether loyalty is weakening.

200
00:09:47,476 --> 00:09:52,626
Toyota's sales of icons like the Prado
and Kluger fell hard in 2026 and some

201
00:09:52,676 --> 00:09:55,826
of those buyers will have discovered
other brands while they waited.

202
00:09:55,876 --> 00:09:59,806
Once a buyer tries something else and
likes it, they don't always come back.

203
00:09:59,856 --> 00:10:01,746
And the brand count keeps growing.

204
00:10:01,796 --> 00:10:05,126
Pappas himself has pointed out that
"In the past five years the number of

205
00:10:05,176 --> 00:10:08,836
brands sold in Australia has grown
significantly" there are now nearly

206
00:10:08,886 --> 00:10:09,426
seventy.

207
00:10:09,476 --> 00:10:12,986
Every one of those brands is
fighting for a slice of the same pie.

208
00:10:13,036 --> 00:10:14,906
Even if Toyota keeps its customers,

209
00:10:14,956 --> 00:10:18,856
it will be very hard to win back the
twenty per cent share it had in 2025.

210
00:10:19,848 --> 00:10:22,468
Now, the case for the
defence and it's strong.

211
00:10:22,518 --> 00:10:27,678
First, Toyota says most of the 2026
drop was about supply, not demand.

212
00:10:27,728 --> 00:10:33,358
The new-generation RAV4 was delayed
from August 2025 to April 2026,

213
00:10:33,408 --> 00:10:36,558
and there was no full HiLux
production run in the first quarter.

214
00:10:36,608 --> 00:10:38,188
In July, John Pappas said:

215
00:10:38,238 --> 00:10:42,478
"This has predominantly been a production
and stock related issue." He added:

216
00:10:42,528 --> 00:10:46,578
"We don't determine global production."
Toyota secured two extra shipments

217
00:10:46,628 --> 00:10:49,248
totalling 30,000 vehicles,
and at one stage,

218
00:10:49,298 --> 00:10:52,898
wait times for top-selling
models were three to six months.

219
00:10:52,948 --> 00:10:56,618
A brand with empty showrooms because it
can't get enough cars is not the same

220
00:10:56,668 --> 00:10:58,378
as a brand nobody wants.

221
00:10:58,428 --> 00:11:00,078
There's another telling statistic.

222
00:11:00,128 --> 00:11:01,858
In early 2026,

223
00:11:01,908 --> 00:11:05,618
accounting firm BDO reported that a
typical Toyota dealer was selling around

224
00:11:05,668 --> 00:11:09,258
72 new cars a month the
highest of any major brand.

225
00:11:09,308 --> 00:11:11,658
A BYD dealer averaged 48.

226
00:11:11,708 --> 00:11:14,938
Toyota's network is still the
most productive in the country.

227
00:11:14,988 --> 00:11:17,058
Second, the numbers are already turning.

228
00:11:17,108 --> 00:11:21,338
In September, the RAV4 was the
second-best-selling vehicle in the country,

229
00:11:21,388 --> 00:11:25,318
with 4,624 sales up 81 per cent.

230
00:11:25,368 --> 00:11:30,218
In October, Pappas said Toyota's order
books were still "very, very healthy".

231
00:11:30,268 --> 00:11:33,338
Asked whether Toyota was planning
for decline, he said: "No.

232
00:11:33,388 --> 00:11:34,958
